This course-owned probe checks only the account phase of the dedicated erudior-desk PAM service. It does not perform an interactive password authentication. It never changes identity or opens a user session. Save as pam-account.c in a private build directory.

*Transcript lines 1–21.*

Compile with `gcc -Wall -Wextra -o pam-account pam-account.c -lpam`. From this trusted local build run `sudo ./pam-account desklearner` and `sudo ./pam-account deskvisitor`. Record each exit status separately. Check expiry and baseline account status before blaming the list rule; the second required pam_unix module also participates. Delete only the dedicated service and its list when done, or restore the snapshot.

Station, after saving pam-account.c and setting up the dedicated service and allowlist.

*Transcript lines 22–27.*

The transcript uses `>` for typed input. Follow the host and branch directions above. Output excerpts show one run. Identifiers, times and package versions can vary.

```plaintext
>cat > pam-account.c <<'ERUDIOR_FILE'
>#include <security/pam_appl.h>
>#include <stdio.h>
>static int no_prompt(int n, const struct pam_message **m,
>                     struct pam_response **r, void *data) {
>    (void)n; (void)m; (void)r; (void)data;
>    return PAM_CONV_ERR;
>}
>int main(int argc, char **argv) {
>    if (argc != 2) return 2;
>    struct pam_conv conv = { no_prompt, NULL };
>    pam_handle_t *handle = NULL;
>    int rc = pam_start("erudior-desk", argv[1], &conv, &handle);
>    if (rc != PAM_SUCCESS) return 3;
>    rc = pam_acct_mgmt(handle, 0);
>    puts(pam_strerror(handle, rc));
>    pam_end(handle, rc);
>    return rc == PAM_SUCCESS ? 0 : 1;
>}
>ERUDIOR_FILE

>gcc -Wall -Wextra -o pam-account pam-account.c -lpam

>sudo ./pam-account desklearner
Success
>sudo ./pam-account deskvisitor
Permission denied
```
